Description: Recognise operators added in Twig 2.12 to 3.23
 twig-lexer 1.0.0 predates these operators and splits or misreads them:
 "===" became "==" + "=", "?." became "?" + ".", and "xor", "has some" and
 "has every" became names. Added, each verified against Twig's tagged
 sources (Extension/CoreExtension.php operator list):
   <=>                      Twig 2.12.0 (all levels)
   has some, has every      Twig 3.5.0
   xor                      Twig 3.15.0
   ?:  (single token)       Twig 3.17.0
   ===, !==, ?.             Twig 3.23.0
 Operators that exist only in Twig 3 are added at level 3, as upstream
 already does for the spread operator.
Author: Numiko
Origin: other, https://github.com/twigphp/Twig/blob/v3.28.0/src/Extension/CoreExtension.php
Forwarded: not-needed
Twig-Reference: 2.12.0, 3.5.0, 3.15.0, 3.17.0, 3.23.0
Test: test/fixtures/twig/operators.twig and tags.twig (parity with Twig 3.28.0); npm run test:upstream
Last-Update: 2026-09-23
---
diff --git a/src/main/lib/Lexer.ts b/src/main/lib/Lexer.ts
index a00919c..1fc64c8 100644
--- a/src/main/lib/Lexer.ts
+++ b/src/main/lib/Lexer.ts
@@ -186,9 +186,14 @@ export class Lexer {
             '//',
             '%',
             '**',
-            '??'
+            '??',
+            '<=>'
         ];
 
+        if (this.level === 3) {
+            this.operators.push('xor', 'has some', 'has every', '?:', '===', '!==', '?.');
+        }
+
         this.tagPair = ['{%', '%}'];
         this.commentPair = ['{#', '#}'];
         this.interpolationPair = ['#{', '}'];
